        "title": "Microsoft FY27 Q1 · Cloud growth and delivery",
        "html": "<style>body{margin:0;background:#f3f6fa;color:#18324b;font-family:Arial,sans-serif}*{box-sizing:border-box}.wrap{padding:44px}h1{font-size:42px;font-weight:750;margin:12px 0}h2{font-size:25px;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 16px}p{font-size:19px;line-height:1.45}.kicker{font-size:15px;letter-spacing:1.6px;color:#536b82}.muted{font-size:16px;color:#536b82}.hero{background:#153c60;color:white;padding:26px;border-radius:14px;margin:24px 0}.panel{background:white;border:1px solid #dce5ee;padding:24px;border-radius:14px}.metric{font-size:36px;color:#126e9e;font-weight:700}td{font-size:18px;padding:12px 8px!important}.watch{background:#fff3e8;border-left:5px solid #b66a35;padding:24px;border-radius:10px}.qa+ .qa{border-top:1px solid #dce5ee;margin-top:20px;padding-top:20px}.tag{display:inline-block;background:#e8f4ed;color:#286347;padding:4px 10px;border-radius:6px;font-size:14px}.strip{background:#e9f0f7;padding:18px;border-radius:9px;margin-top:20px}</style><main class='wrap'><div class='kicker'>MICROSOFT FY27 Q1 · LECTURE SYNTHESIS</div><h1>Cloud growth meets capacity limits</h1><p class='muted'>Source cited: October 28, 2026 investor call. Actuals cover the quarter ended September 30; guidance covers the FY27 December quarter unless otherwise stated. Expectations are dated October 28, not completed results.</p><section class='hero'><h2>Strong actuals; ambitious, execution-sensitive guidance</h2><p class='mb-0'>Demand exceeds supply. Better fleet efficiency supports capacity monetization, but delivery depends on further engineering progress, capacity timing, token economics and demand conversion.</p></section><div class='row g-4'><div class='col-6'><section class='panel h-100'><h2>Reported September-quarter actuals</h2><div class='metric'>$92.3B revenue</div><p class='muted'>Up 19% year-over-year</p><table class='table mb-0'><tbody><tr><td>Operating income</td><td><b>$45.9B · +21%</b></td></tr><tr><td>Operating margin</td><td><b>50%</b></td></tr><tr><td>Microsoft Cloud</td><td><b>$63.9B · +30%</b></td></tr><tr><td>Azure growth</td><td><b>48%</b></td></tr><tr><td>Commercial RPO</td><td><b>$677B · +73%</b></td></tr></tbody></table></section></div><div class='col-6'><section class='panel h-100'><h2>December-quarter expectations</h2><div class='metric'>$96–$97.1B revenue</div><p class='muted'>Expected growth: 18–19%</p><table class='table mb-0'><tbody><tr><td>Azure growth</td><td><b>49–50%<br>constant currency</b></td></tr><tr><td>Cloud gross margin</td><td><b>About 65%</b></td></tr><tr><td>Company operating margin</td><td><b>Slightly down YoY</b></td></tr><tr><td>Q2 capex</td><td><b>Above $50B</b></td></tr><tr><td>Calendar-year capex</td><td><b>Approximately $170B</b></td></tr></tbody></table></section></div></div><p class='muted mt-3'>Comparison caution: Azure guidance is explicitly constant currency; the lecture does not specify the currency basis for reported 48% growth.</p><section class='panel mt-4'><h2>Investment intensity and cash generation</h2><div class='row g-3'><div class='col-4'><div class='metric'>$45.2B</div><p>Reported capex; roughly ¾ for short-lived assets, mainly CPUs and GPUs.</p></div><div class='col-4'><div class='metric'>$58B</div><p>Reported operating cash flow.</p></div><div class='col-4'><div class='metric'>$18.6B</div><p>Reported free cash flow.</p></div></div><div class='muted'>FY2027 expectation: remain free cash flow positive despite elevated investment.</div></section><section class='panel mt-4'><h2>Investor Q&A <span class='tag'>Both answered</span></h2><div class='qa'><p><b>Q: As AI capacity comes online, what matters more—Azure utilization and unit cost, or Fabric, Foundry and Copilot?</b></p><p class='mb-0'><b>Satya Nadella:</b> In effect, all of the above. One fungible pool produces returns through efficient operation, demand allocation and reinforcement across the stack.</p></div><div class='qa'><p><b>Q: What supports the 49–50% Azure growth outlook?</b></p><p class='mb-0'><b>Amy Hood:</b> Efficiencies across application, model, GPU, CPU, storage and memory. Those gains helped direct more capacity toward Foundry in the reported quarter; the forecast also needs further engineering progress.</p></div><div class='strip'><b>Reconstructed logic:</b> stack efficiency → more usable capacity and better allocation. This supports growth delivery; it does not remove forecast uncertainty.</div></section><section class='watch mt-4'><h2>Delivery watchpoints—not resolved outcomes</h2><div class='row g-3'><div class='col-6'><p class='mb-0'><b>Supply and timing:</b> demand still exceeds supply. Some third-party deliveries slipped; some third-party GPU capacity moved to the second half.</p></div><div class='col-6'><p class='mb-0'><b>Engineering and economics:</b> further efficiency gains are needed. Token pricing and delayed capacity remain uncertain; demand conversion is part of the concluding delivery test.</p></div></div></section><p class='muted mt-4 mb-0'>No additional unanswered investor question, assigned action, owner or deadline was stated. Watchpoints above are monitoring priorities, not announced commitments.</p></main>"
      }
